{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3212","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3212","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3212\",\"parent_revision\":\"f354afc67fa0b779f1a544d121589049dc567ecff60ff0bfd7c72c8c2fd8ef31\",\"root_revision\":\"f354afc67fa0b779f1a544d121589049dc567ecff60ff0bfd7c72c8c2fd8ef31\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Docs\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nCorrection (2026-08-24, after research): the original description below was wrong about the fix. `EnterWorktree`/`ExitWorktree` are existing first-class harness tools that already solve this \u2014 `EnterWorktree({path: ...})` explicitly documents entering a worktree already created with `git worktree add`, and switches the session's own cwd into it (no manual absolute-path-threading into subagent prompts needed). The only genuinely non-obvious, repo-specific gap is a single interaction: this repo's `worktree.baseRef: \\\"head\\\"` setting (`.claude/settings.json`) governs `EnterWorktree({name: ...})`, so a task needing a different base (e.g. `origin/main` for a PR unrelated to current HEAD) must create the worktree manually first, then `EnterWorktree({path: ...})` to enter it \u2014 `name=` alone can't select a different base ref. That is a 2-4 line cache-worthy addition to `.claude/rules/commit-cadence-and-worktrees.md`, not a new rule section, and skill-sync's own SKILL.md should NOT get a speculative new pipeline stage for this until an isolation-mode design is actually run and validated once, not authored from first principles.\\n\\nOriginal (pre-research) description, kept for the reproduction context \u2014 do not re-derive its \\\"manually creating a worktree\\\" framing as the answer, it's superseded by the correction above:\\n\\n---\\n\\nGoal: give any skill or pipeline whose write stage should land in an isolated worktree and open a PR (rather than edit the current checkout in place) a single documented pattern to follow, instead of each skill inventing its own ad hoc handling.\\n\\nReproduction / observation: running `/plugin-creator:skill-sync` against `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/agentskills/` this session, the user asked mid-run for the sync's writes to land in a worktree with a PR opened from it. Nothing in `.claude/rules/commit-cadence-and-worktrees.md` or in skill-sync's own pipeline (`plugins/plugin-creator/skills/skill-sync/SKILL.md`) covered this case, and the orchestrator improvised: manually created a worktree, then hand-threaded absolute worktree paths into every subagent prompt instead of checking for a harness-native mechanism first.\\n\\nImpact: without the correct pointer, future sessions will re-derive this from scratch, with the same risk of missing `EnterWorktree` and reinventing manual path-threading.\\n\\nUser story: as an orchestrating session, when a user or a skill's own contract calls for isolated writes that ship as a PR, I want to know to reach for `EnterWorktree` and its `path=` entry mode, so I don't hand-roll git commands and manual path contracts under time pressure.\\n\\nPayoff: a two-to-four-line addition to the existing worktree rule file, not a new large rule or an unvalidated pipeline change.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: skill-sync agentskills sync session, 2026-08-24\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"e44a56758032067535cc37b7ff340e763a1431c1ce29acf9ce0320034a46ae9b\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQUHV7w\"}"}